# Raspberry Pi 5 Memory & Performance Optimization Summary

This document summarizes all optimizations implemented for your Raspberry Pi 5 running a real-time robotics platform in a Docker container.

## 1. RAM Disk Creation

### Commands:
```bash
sudo mkdir -p /mnt/ramdisk
sudo mount -t tmpfs -o size=4G tmpfs /mnt/ramdisk
echo "tmpfs /mnt/ramdisk tmpfs size=4G,mode=1777 0 0" | sudo tee -a /etc/fstab
```

### Purpose:
Creates a 4GB filesystem that exists entirely in RAM rather than on disk. This provides ultra-fast storage for temporary files with microsecond access times rather than millisecond access times of SD cards or SSDs. The entry in `/etc/fstab` ensures this RAM disk is recreated on system boot.

## 2. RAM-Based Logging

### Commands:
```bash
sudo mkdir -p /var/log/ramlogs
sudo mount -t tmpfs -o size=1G tmpfs /var/log/ramlogs
echo "tmpfs /var/log/ramlogs tmpfs size=1G,mode=0755 0 0" | sudo tee -a /etc/fstab
```

### Purpose:
Creates a 1GB RAM-based filesystem specifically for logs. This eliminates SD card wear from frequent log writes and removes I/O-related jitter that could affect real-time performance. Since robotics applications often generate significant logs, this prevents disk I/O from introducing timing inconsistencies.

## 3. System Memory Parameter Optimizations

### Commands:
```bash
sudo sysctl -w vm.swappiness=1
sudo sysctl -w vm.vfs_cache_pressure=50
sudo sysctl -w vm.dirty_ratio=60
sudo sysctl -w vm.dirty_background_ratio=30
sudo sysctl -w vm.overcommit_memory=1

echo "vm.swappiness=1" | sudo tee -a /etc/sysctl.conf
echo "vm.vfs_cache_pressure=50" | sudo tee -a /etc/sysctl.conf
echo "vm.dirty_ratio=60" | sudo tee -a /etc/sysctl.conf
echo "vm.dirty_background_ratio=30" | sudo tee -a /etc/sysctl.conf
echo "vm.overcommit_memory=1" | sudo tee -a /etc/sysctl.conf

sudo sysctl -p
```

### Purpose:
- `vm.swappiness=1`: Tells the kernel to avoid swapping to disk unless absolutely necessary
- `vm.vfs_cache_pressure=50`: Encourages the kernel to keep directory and inode information in memory
- `vm.dirty_ratio=60`: Allows more dirty pages (modified data) to stay in memory
- `vm.dirty_background_ratio=30`: Sets a higher threshold before background writeback begins
- `vm.overcommit_memory=1`: Always allows memory allocation requests, beneficial for RAM-heavy applications
- Adding to `/etc/sysctl.conf` makes these changes persist across reboots

## 4. Huge Pages Configuration

### Commands:
```bash
echo always | sudo tee /sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/enabled
echo always | sudo tee /sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/defrag

echo 'echo always > /sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/enabled' | sudo tee -a /etc/rc.local
echo 'echo always > /sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/defrag' | sudo tee -a /etc/rc.local
sudo chmod +x /etc/rc.local
```

### Purpose:
Enables transparent huge pages which reduce TLB (Translation Lookaside Buffer) misses and improve memory access performance. When enabled, the system uses larger memory pages (2MB instead of 4KB), reducing overhead for memory-intensive applications. The changes to `/etc/rc.local` ensure these settings persist across reboots.

## 5. Creating Optimized Docker Container

### Commands:
```bash
sudo docker stop MentorPi
sudo docker commit MentorPi mentorpi_realtime_backup:v1_ram_optimized
sudo docker run -d \
  --name MentorPi_RAM \
  --privileged \
  --network host \
  --cap-add CAP_SYS_NICE \
  -v /dev:/dev \
  -v /tmp/.X11-unix:/tmp/.X11-unix \
  -v /home/pi/docker/tmp:/home/ubuntu/shared \
  -v /home/pi/dev:/home/ubuntu/dev \
  -v /mnt/ramdisk:/mnt/ramdisk \
  -v /var/log/ramlogs:/var/log/robot_logs \
  --security-opt label=disable \
  --ulimit memlock=-1:-1 \
  --ulimit rtprio=99:99 \
  --shm-size=2g \
  -e DISPLAY=:0 \
  --user ubuntu \
  --restart always \
  mentorpi_realtime_backup:v1_ram_optimized \
  tail -f /dev/null
```

### Purpose:
Creates an optimized container with:
- RAM disk mounts for fast storage and logging
- Unlimited locked memory (`memlock=-1:-1`) for real-time applications
- High real-time priority capabilities (`rtprio=99:99`)
- Increased shared memory size (`shm-size=2g`) for inter-process communication
- All the existing mounts and capabilities needed for your robotics application


sudo bash -c 'cat > /opt/ros/humble/setup.bash << EOF
#!/bin/bash

# Set ROS 2 memory optimization environment variables
export RMW_IMPLEMENTATION=rmw_cyclonedds_cpp
export CYCLONEDDS_URI="<CycloneDDS><Domain><General><SharedMemory>true</SharedMemory><NetworkInterfaceAddress>lo</NetworkInterfaceAddress></General></Domain></CycloneDDS>"
export RMW_IMPLEMENTATION_SETTINGS="-DCycloneDDS_IDLC_USE_ZEROCOPY=ON"
export CYCLONEDDS_RT="LIFO,BATCH"
export TMPDIR="/mnt/ramdisk/robot_temp"
export ROS_LOG_DIR="/var/log/robot_logs/robot"

# Create necessary directories
mkdir -p /mnt/ramdisk/robot_temp
mkdir -p /var/log/robot_logs/robot

# source setup.sh from same directory as this file
AMENT_SHELL=bash
AMENT_CURRENT_PREFIX=\$(builtin cd "\`dirname "\${BASH_SOURCE[0]}"\`" && pwd)
# trace output
if [ -n "\$AMENT_TRACE_SETUP_FILES" ]; then
  echo "# . \\"\$AMENT_CURRENT_PREFIX/setup.sh\\""
fi
. "\$AMENT_CURRENT_PREFIX/setup.sh"
EOF'

exit
sudo docker commit MentorPi mentorpi_realtime_backup:v1_ram_ros_optimized
sudo docker restart MentorPi
```

### Purpose:
Modifies the ROS 2 Humble setup file to add memory optimizations that benefit all ROS nodes:
- `RMW_IMPLEMENTATION=rmw_cyclonedds_cpp`: Uses CycloneDDS middleware which is more memory-efficient than the default
- `CYCLONEDDS_URI`: Configures CycloneDDS to use shared memory transport and loopback interface only
- `RMW_IMPLEMENTATION_SETTINGS`: Enables zero-copy optimization to reduce memory copying overhead
- `CYCLONEDDS_RT`: Prioritizes deterministic communication over throughput
- Also sets temporary and log directories to use RAM

## 9. Monitoring Tools

### Commands:
```bash
# Install monitoring tools
sudo apt install htop sysstat

# Monitor temperature
vcgencmd measure_temp
watch -n 1 vcgencmd measure_temp

# Monitor throttling
vcgencmd get_throttled

# Monitor memory usage
watch -n 1 free -m
watch -n 5 "cat /proc/meminfo | grep -E 'Cache|Dirty|PageTables|AnonPages'"

# Monitor RAM disk usage
watch -n 5 "df -h | grep tmpfs"

# Monitor I/O operations
iostat -x 5
```

### Purpose:
These commands help monitor system performance and ensure optimizations are working as expected:
- Temperature monitoring to ensure the Raspberry Pi 5 stays below the 80°C throttling threshold
- Memory usage monitoring to verify RAM utilization
- RAM disk usage monitoring to ensure they don't fill up
- I/O monitoring to verify reduced disk activity

